<article class="shell-article">
  <nav class="shell-breadcrumb"><a href="/hc/en-us">Help Center</a> / What is biogas?</nav>

  <p>Biogas is a renewable energy source formed when organic material – for example livestock manure, food scraps and residual products from agriculture and industry – breaks down without oxygen. The process is called <strong>anaerobic digestion</strong> and takes place in large, sealed tanks at a biogas plant. The result is a methane-rich gas that can be upgraded to <strong>biomethane</strong> of the same quality as ordinary natural gas.</p>

  <div class="shell-callout">
    <strong>In short:</strong> Biogas is waste turned into energy. The same methane that would otherwise leak into the atmosphere from manure and waste is captured and used as green fuel – and the residue is returned to the fields as fertiliser.
  </div>

  <h2>Why is biogas green?</h2>
  <p>When organic waste breaks down naturally, it releases methane – a powerful greenhouse gas. At a biogas plant this methane is captured instead and replaces fossil natural gas. The CO₂ released when biogas is burned is part of the natural cycle, because it comes from plants and animals and not from fossil sources that have been locked underground for millions of years. That is why biomethane is considered <strong>CO₂-neutral</strong>.</p>

  <h2>What can biogas be used for?</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Heat and electricity:</strong> Biomethane is fed into the existing gas grid and heats Danish homes and businesses.</li>
    <li><strong>Industry:</strong> Green gas replaces fossil energy in production where high temperatures are hard to electrify.</li>
    <li><strong>Transport:</strong> Compressed and liquefied biogas (BioLNG) can power trucks, buses and ships.</li>
    <li><strong>Fertiliser:</strong> The digested biomass is a valuable, low-odour fertiliser product for agriculture.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Biogas in Denmark</h2>
  <p>Denmark is among the world's leading countries in biogas. A steadily growing share of the gas flowing through the Danish gas grid is green. Through its 2023 acquisition of <a href="/hc/en-us/p/shell-og-nature-energy">Nature Energy</a>, Shell has become one of the largest players in the market, with a wide range of plants spread across the country.</p>

